WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:06.390
Speaker 1: Hey, guys, I don't know how Joe
Biden did this, but there are more people

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:14.670
working in the United States today than at
any previous time in the history of the country,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.720
if you can believe that.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.820
Obama now.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.200
This is, of course, very tongue in cheek.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.330
But it is also true if you're new to this
show, I really want you to pay special attention

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:33.430
to this segment because this is actually I
really want to do this the right way.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.430
Okay.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:41.480
We started seeing headlines late last week
like this one record number of Americans employed

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:52.960
in December, 159,244,000 record number of
Americans employed in December.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:59.870
Now, this is one of my favorite political
footballs, depending on whether you want to

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:06.400
argue the economy is good or the economy is
bad for politically partizan reasons, you

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:13.070
can very often use phrases like there are
more Americans than ever currently working

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:18.140
or there are more Americans than ever currently
not working.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.540
Or you can go away from the number and talk
about the percentage.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:29.390
You can say the labor participation rate is
higher than ever or the rate is lower than

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.390
ever.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.390
Okay.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.689
This is all basically B.S. in a vacuum.

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:38.780
So let's really break down what's going on
here in the United States.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.770
The population continues to increase.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:49.110
And so if over time there is an average labor
participation rate, let's just call it 60%.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.110
Okay.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:55.150
One of the things that will be true is that
the number of people working and the number

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:58.630
of people not working will both be at record
highs.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:01:59.630
Think through the numbers.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.630
It's very simple.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.579
In a country of 100 million people with a
60% labor participation rate, you would have

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:10.330
60 million people working and 40 million people
not working.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.410
Great.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.620
Time goes by.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.230
You get to 200 million people in the population.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.410
That's 60%.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.520
Labor participation rate now means, wow, we
have 120 million people working.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.209
We have a record number of people working,
but we also have 80 million people not working.

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:29.170
We have a record number of people not working.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.640
Is the economy good or is the economy bad?

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.640
I don't know.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.590
Depends who, what, what point you want to
make or what you want to justify.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.860
So it's not actually a great indicator of
how the economy is doing.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:48.909
But what's ironic, what's funny, what's interesting
is that Republicans love to take either the

00:02:48.909 --> 00:02:54.340
number of people working or the labor participation
rate out of context to make the case that

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.640
the economy is good or bad.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.440
Now, let me show you a couple of different
things here.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.530
This is a chart of the labor participation
rate, as you can see.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.610
This goes back before 1950.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.610
I'll describe it to you.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.470
It's not as high as it's ever been.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:14.080
Right now, the labor participation rate is
between 62 and 63%.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.480
In the late nineties, early 2000s, the labor
participation rate was 67%.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:24.560
So right now, if you were one of the politically
partizan people that wants to say the economy

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:31.450
is bad, you go, we are a full five points
lower in terms of the labor participation

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.530
rate than we were 20 years ago.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.090
It's terrible right now.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.090
Okay.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.090
That's one perspective.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.700
However, if instead of looking at the rate
we look at the number of people working, which

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:48.510
is what that headline I showed you says you
would accurately be able to say there are

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:53.060
more people than ever working right now.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.739
That being said, you would also have to figure
out if there are more people than ever not

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:00.090
working right now because of the growth of
the population.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.239
So let me be very real with you.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:03.239
Okay.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.629
In order to figure out how the economy is
doing, you have to look at many factors and

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:09.610
you should be looking at the employment rate.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.760
You should be looking at job creation.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.560
It's useful to look at stock market performance
to a degree.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.019
Consumer confidence.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:18.209
Look at inflation.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.650
Look at wages.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.989
Look at export.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:21.989
Import balance.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:22.989
At trade balance.

00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:25.670
There's a bunch of different things that we
would look at.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:30.729
One of the problems with the labor participation
rate or the number of people working is how

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:32.020
it is composed.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.600
So a lot of times right wingers love to say
the unemployment rate isn't accurate because

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.680
if you stop looking for work, you no longer
count as unemployed.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.710
That's true, but that's always been the case.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.810
That was the case when Reagan was president.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.389
And those very same Republicans talked about
how great the unemployment rate was.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:50.389
That's always the case.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:52.600
That's a limitation of the of the measurement.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.539
The problem with the labor participation rate
is sometimes when people aren't working, it's

00:04:57.539 --> 00:04:58.760
because the economy is good.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.729
I'm going to give you some examples.

00:05:00.729 --> 00:05:01.729
Okay.

00:05:01.729 --> 00:05:06.930
If you are a high school junior or senior
or in college, meaning you're like between

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.830
16 and 22 years old.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:09.830
Okay.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.039
If you're in college full time.

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:17.460
In these numbers, you show up as someone who's
not working, you show up as you're in the

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.479
not working part.

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:25.389
But the reason you might be able to afford
to go to college full time is because the

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:30.340
economy is good and your parents work and
they don't need you to work to help feed the

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:31.639
family.

00:05:31.639 --> 00:05:33.580
You could also look at the inverse.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.850
You could have a higher labor participation
because all of these people who could be in

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:41.789
college have to work because the economy is
so bad.

00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:45.419
Their household with their parents needs more
income.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:47.250
I'll give you another example.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.380
Stay at home, parents.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.370
Lower the labor participation rate.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:57.490
But if the economy were really bad, a lot
of those stay at home parents would have to

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.280
go out and get a job.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.479
So stay at home.

00:06:00.479 --> 00:06:05.830
Parents make this number look worse, but it's
a sign that the economy is better.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.169
I'll give you another example.

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:10.449
When people retire, they exit the labor force.

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:13.280
They show up on that not working side.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.910
If the economy is good, more people can afford
to retire.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.180
If the economy is bad.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:24.139
More of those people who would like to retire,
can't they keep working?

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:28.160
You've got a higher labor participation, but
not because the economy is good, because it's

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.160
bad.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.160
So you get the picture.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.240
It's a very deceptive number by itself.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.590
Republicans love using it.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:34.630
What's the truth of the matter?

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:37.650
The truth of the matter is the economy is
pretty good right now.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.230
The stock market is sort of stabilizing.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.810
Inflation is coming down.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.980
Job creation numbers are better than expected.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.810
Unemployment's low.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.259
Is it a perfect economy?

00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:48.259
Of course not.

00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:49.259
Is there suffering?

00:06:49.259 --> 00:06:50.259
Is there poverty?

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:51.259
Is there hunger?

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:52.430
Of course there's all of those things.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.860
But by the metrics we normally use, the economy
is doing pretty well now.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.040
One last thing on this.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.501
Joe Biden's approval rating does seem to be
responding to a lot of different things, but

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:05.720
it's basically been an increasing approval
rating since late July for Joe Biden.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:07.080
So late July.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.810
Biden's approval bottomed out.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.550
It then spiked by early September.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:16.020
And it's been just like a slow burn increase
to close to 44%.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.259
Is 44% an amazing approval rating?

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:20.259
No.

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:25.790
In the modern era of hyper partizanship, is
it a typical approval rating for a president

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:28.100
who's doing the job well enough?

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:31.770
Yeah, it's sad that our standards are that
low.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.560
But it is it's certainly no disaster.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.280
And it will be interesting to see how that
impacts the coming two years.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.560
So more people than ever working.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.400
Is it the be all end all of judging the economy?

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.150
It is not.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.110
But I hope you all give me credit.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:08:40.149
I don't play the partizan games with the labor
force 

00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:53.360
participation rate.

